  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 137 AM MDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nThe synoptic pattern does not change much for Thursday, but the \ninverted trough looks to move further to the southeast, potentially \ninto TX. This should bring the coverage of showers/storms down a \ntouch, but remnant moisture within the state looks to keep isolated \nto locally scattered activity around. As has been the case, the \nprimary threat will continue to be gusty winds, with low chances for \naccumulation above 0.1\". The upper level flow begins to become more \nzonal into Friday and the weekend as attention turns to a robust low \npressure developing in the PNW along with the likely remnants of \nHurricane Rachel. How this trough digs through western CONUS through \nthe weekend and interacts with the remnants of Rachel is the focus \npoint for a potential more active period of weather. The current \nfavored trajectory of the systems brings the low pressure down \ntowards CA and NV, with Rachel's remnants being absorbed along the \ntrough axis and ejected into the Desert Southwest. At this time, \nprecipitation is primarily favored over western NM through the late \nweekend and into next week. Orographic forcing is likely to enhance \nrainfall amounts across western NM. Currently, heavier precipitation \nlooks to remain primarily in AZ, however subtle shifts in the \nplacement and movement of each feature can allow for the location \nof heavier precipitation to move. Keep an eye on the forecast as \nwe approach next weekend.",
